  "name": "BLOOD UREA NITROGEN (BUN)",
  "code": "1044",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"344\"><strong data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"186\">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)</strong> measures the amount of nitrogen in the blood that comes from <strong data-start=\"248\" data-end=\"256\">urea</strong>, a waste product of protein metabolism formed in the liver and excreted by the kidneys.</p>\n<p data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"376\">This test is commonly used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"378\" data-end=\"661\">\n<li data-start=\"378\" data-end=\"408\">\n<p data-start=\"380\" data-end=\"408\"><strong data-start=\"380\" data-end=\"408\">Evaluate kidney function</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"409\" data-end=\"439\">\n<p data-start=\"411\" data-end=\"439\">Monitor <strong data-start=\"419\" data-end=\"439\">hydration status</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"505\">\n<p data-start=\"442\" data-end=\"505\">Assess response to <strong data-start=\"461\" data-end=\"473\">dialysis</strong> or treatment for kidney disease</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"506\" data-end=\"598\">\n<p data-start=\"508\" data-end=\"598\">Help differentiate between <strong data-start=\"535\" data-end=\"569\">prerenal, renal, and postrenal</strong> causes of kidney dysfunction</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"599\" data-end=\"661\">\n<p data-start=\"601\" data-end=\"661\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"610\" data-end=\"632\">protein metabolism</strong> or liver function indirectly</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Elevated BUN (Azotemia) :&nbsp;</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prerenal causes</strong></td>\n<td>Dehydration, hypovolemia, heart failure &mdash; &darr; renal perfusion leads to BUN retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Renal causes</strong></td>\n<td>Acute or chronic kidney disease, glomerulonephritis, tubular necrosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Postrenal causes</strong></td>\n<td>Obstruction of urinary tract (e.g., stones, BPH, tumors)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High protein diet or GI bleed</strong></td>\n<td>Increased protein breakdown leads to more urea production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Catabolic states</strong></td>\n<td>Fever, stress, corticosteroid therapy, or severe infections may increase BUN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Upper GI hemorrhage</strong></td>\n<td>Breakdown of blood proteins in the gut increases BUN significantly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"663\" data-end=\"758\">&nbsp;</p>",
